Java Memory Allocation

1]What is Memory Allocation?
	When a Java program runs, the JVM needs memory to store:
Variables
Objects
Methods
Classes
Arrays
Strings
Method calls
Static data

The JVM divides memory into different areas.

The most important areas for beginners are:

JVM Memory
│
├── Stack - stores method call frames, local variables, partial results; one stack per thread
│
├── Heap - stores all objects and instance variables; shared across threads
│
├── Method Area - stores class-level data: class structure, static variables, method bytecode, constant pool
│
├── PC (Program Counter) Register — holds the address of the currently executing JVM instruction; one per thread
│
└── Native Method Stack — supports native (non-Java, e.g., C/C++ via JNI) method calls

Note: Actual variable/object memory allocation happens primarily during runtime, not when you compile the Java file.

