3]Stack Overflow:
	Because stack memory is limited, excessive method calls can cause: StackOverflowError
Ex:
class Demo {
    static void test() {
        test();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        test();
    }
}
What happens:
main()
 ↓
test()
 ↓
test()
 ↓
test()
 ↓
test()
 ↓
...

Stack keep growing
┌──────────┐
│ test()   │
├──────────┤
│ test()   │
├──────────┤
│ test()   │
├──────────┤
│ test()   │
├──────────┤
│ main()   │
└──────────┘
Eventually -> StackOverflowError

4]Heap Overflow
	Heap is where objects and arrays are allocated.
If your program keeps creating objects and they remain reachable:
Ex:
while (true) {
    Student s = new Student();
}
Depending on what remains reachable and JVM behavior, excessive heap usage can eventually result in:
OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space

5]Stack vs Heap — thread safety
 - Each thread has its own separate stack, so local variables are inherently thread-safe — no other thread can access another thread's stack. 
 - The heap is shared across all threads, so objects on the heap are not thread-safe by default and require explicit synchronization if accessed concurrently.

6]How does Java decide to remove an object from heap?
 - The Garbage Collector identifies objects that are no longer reachable 
 — i.e., no live reference points to them from GC roots (active threads, static references, local variables in call stack). 
 - Once unreachable, the object becomes eligible for garbage collection and its memory is reclaimed.

Classes and Objects

1]Objects:
	• Object is the primary unit of Object-Oriented Programming.
	• It represents the real-life entities. Example: pen, chair, table, computer, watch, etc.,
	• It can be physical or logical.
	• An object has three characteristics:
		1. State: represents data or value stored in an object.
		2. Behavior: represents the behavior or functionality of an object. This function is used to manipulate the data and interact with other objects
		3. Identity: It gives unique name to an object. Each object is identified in Java by unique memory location.
2]Class: 
	• Class is a template or blueprint of the objects.
	• It defines the state (variables) and behaviour (methods) common to all objects of a certain kind.
	• It is used to create object instances.
	
3]Access  Modifiers:
	• Access modifiers or specifiers defines the scope and accessibility of data and method in class.
	• There are three Java access modifiers:
		1. public: accessible in all class in your application.
		2. protected: accessible within the class in which it is defined and in its subclass(es).
		3. private: accessible only within the class in which it is defined.
		4. default (declared/defined without using any modifier) : accessible within same class and package
		within which its class is defined.
Note:
• For classes and interface, you can use either public or default
• For attributes, methods and constructors, you can use the one of the following: Private, Protected, Public

Constructors

1]Definition:
	- In java, Constructor is a special method that is executed automatically whenever an instance of object is created. 
	- It is  used to initialize the object and give initial values for object 	attributes. 
Rules for Constructor: 
  • Constructor has same name as class name.
    [Java needs a way to distinguish a constructor from a normal method]
  • Constructor do not have return value but can take arguments.
    [Because a constructor's job is to initialize a newly created object]
    [Java creates the object and calls: The constructor doesn't return the object to you like a normal method]
    [Constructors can receive values just like methods: The constructor uses these values to initialize the object]
  • Constructor cannot be abstract, static, final, and synchronized.
    [static belongs to the class, whereas a constructor is used to initialize an object.]
    [final means something cannot be changed/overridden. Constructors are not inherited by child classes.]
    An abstract method means: "I am declaring a method, but I am not providing its implementation."
    [But constructors must have a body because their purpose is to initialize an object.]
    synchronized is mainly used to control access to methods/blocks when multiple threads are working with the same object/class

2]Types of Constructor
There are two types of constructors in Java:
	1. No-argument/default constructor
	2. Parameterized constructor
Default Constructor
	• A constructor that has no parameters is known as default constructor. It is either user defined or compiler defined constructor.
	• If we don’t create any constructor in a class, then compiler creates default constructor for the class and assign default values to attributes .
	• The user defined default constructor provides user given initial values to attributes of the class  for each instantiation.
Parameterized Constructor
	• A constructor that accepts arguments are known as parameterized constructor. It is use to initialize with your own values.
	• In parameterized constructor, we must provide initial values of objects as arguments to the constructors.
Constructor Overloading
	• More than one constructor with different parameter lists depending on the application. Each constructors functions in its own distinct way.

3]Constructor Chaining:
  - Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor to reuse initialization code.
  - One constructor calls another constructor.
  - Instead of writing the same initialization code in multiple constructors, we can make one constructor call another.
  - There are two types of constructor chaining:
    i)Within the same class → using this() - this() is used to call another constructor of the same class
    ii)Between parent and child classes → using super() - super() calls the parent class constructor

4]this vs super
| `this()`                                        | `super()`                                     |
| ----------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Calls another constructor in the **same class** | Calls constructor of the **parent class**     |
| Constructor chaining within same class          | Constructor chaining between parent and child |
| `this()`                                        | `super()`                                     |
| Must be first statement                         | Must be first statement                       |

this  → refers to the current object instance; used to access instance variables/methods,
super → parent class object/part - super is mainly used when inheritance is involved. - It refers to the immediate parent class.
this() is used to call another constructor in the same class.
super() calls parent constructor

5]Copy Constructor:
  - A copy constructor is a constructor that creates a new object by copying the values from an existing object.
  - Object 1 → copy its values → Object 2
Ex:
class Employee {
    String name;
    int age;
    Employee(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
    // Copy constructor
    Employee(Employee other) {
        this.name = other.name;
        this.age = other.age;
    }
}
Employee e1 = new Employee("John", 30);
Employee e2 = new Employee(e1); // copy of e1

6]Copy constructor — does Java provide one built-in?
 - No, unlike C++, Java doesn't provide a built-in copy constructor. 
 - You must implement it manually if you want to create a new object as a copy of an existing one.

7]Constructor vs method
Constructor						Method
Same name as class				Any valid name
No return type					Has a return type (or void)
Called automatically via new	Called explicitly
Initializes object state		Defines behavior

8]What happens if a subclass constructor doesn't explicitly call super()?
 - The compiler automatically inserts an implicit super() call (no-arg) as the first statement, invoking the parent's no-arg constructor. 
 - If the parent class doesn't have a no-arg constructor (only a parameterized one exists), this causes a compile-time error, 
and you must explicitly call super(args) matching an available parent constructor.

9]Can a constructor call itself repeatedly, or two constructors call each other?
 - No — Java's compiler detects this at compile time and throws an error: recursive constructor invocation. 
 - This applies both to a constructor calling itself directly via this(), and to two constructors calling each other in a cycle 
(A calls B, B calls A) — since it would result in infinite recursion with no base case, similar to a method with no termination condition.
Ex:
Employee() { this(5); }      // ERROR: recursive constructor invocation
Employee(int x) { this(); }

10]Can a constructor be inherited? Can it be overridden?
 - No to both. Constructors are not inherited by subclasses.
 - The reason is simple: a constructor belongs specifically to the class whose object it creates.

Note:
 - Constructors are not inherited and cannot be overridden because they belong to the class in which they are declared. 
 - However, constructors can be overloaded, and a child constructor can call its parent constructor using super(). 
 
11]Can a constructor be private? Use case?
 - Yes. A private constructor prevents instantiation from outside the class
 
Note:
 - Yes, a constructor can be private in Java. A private constructor prevents direct object creation from outside the class. 
 - It is commonly used in Singleton classes, utility classes, factory methods, and when a class wants full control over 
 how its objects are created. 

Static & Non-Static:

1]Static block — when executed?
 - A block of code marked static { } that runs once, when the class is first loaded into memory by the JVM (before main() 
or any object creation) — used to initialize static variables or perform one-time setup.

2]Instance (non-static) block — when executed?
 - A block of code { } (no static keyword) inside a class that runs every time an object is created, right before the constructor body executes.

3]Multiple static blocks
 - Yes, allowed. They execute in the order they appear in the source code, top to bottom, as if they were merged into one.
 
4]Does static block run every object creation or only once?
 - Only once — when the class is loaded by the ClassLoader, regardless of how many objects are subsequently created.

5]Static block vs static method
 - Static block — runs automatically at class loading time, no explicit call needed
 - Static method — must be explicitly invoked (ClassName.method()); doesn't run automatically

6]Can you access instance variables inside a static block?
 - No — a static block runs at class loading time, before any object exists. 
 - Since instance variables belong to objects (which don't exist yet), the compiler throws an error if you try to 
access them directly inside a static context.

7]Do objects share static variables?
	- 	Yes — static variables belong to the class, not to individual objects, so all instances share the same single copy. 
Changing it through one object reflects across all others.
Ex:
class Counter {
    static int count = 0;
    Counter() { count++; }
}
Counter c1 = new Counter(); // count = 1
Counter c2 = new Counter(); // count = 2 (shared, not separate per object)

Encapsulation:

1]Definition:
	• Encapsulation is a another important concept of Object Oriented Programming.
	• It is the process of binding the data and behaviour into a single unit called class.
Need of Encapsulation:
	• In this world, many data are sensitive, confidential and personal. Hence privacy is an important threat with respect to every data.
	• Data privacy is achieved with the help of encapsulation concept in Java.
Achieve encapsulation in Java:
	• Encapsulation can be achieved by declaring data in the class members as private.
	• Provide public setter and getter methods to modify and view the private class members. (Good Practice)
Note:
	• Encapsulation prevents the private class members being accessed by external classes and
	methods. Therefore, it is also known as data hiding.

2]Setter and getter Methods:
	- It is used to update and retrieve the value of variables.
Rules for setter Method:
	• It should be public, if it needs to be accessed from outside.
	• The return-type should be void.
	• The setter method should be prefixed with set.
	• It should take some argument i.e. it should not be no-argument method.
Rules for getter Method:
	• It should be public, if it needs to be accessed from outside
	• The return-type should not be void i.e. according to our requirement we have to give return-type.
	• The getter method should be prefixed with get.
	• It should not take any argument.

Inheritance:

1]Introduction
 - Inheritance is the OOP mechanism where one class (child/subclass/derived) acquires the properties and behaviors of 
 another class (parent/superclass/base), using the extends keyword.
 - Inheritance represents the IS-A relationship which is also known as a parent-child relationship.
 
Subclass/Child class:
 - Subclass is a class which inherits the other class. It is also called a derived class, extended class, or child class.
 - Child classes will have the properties of parent class and its own attributes and behaviors that are unique to it as well.
Super Class/Parent Class: 
 - Super class is the class from where a subclass inherits the features. It is also called a base class or a parent class.
 
2]Why do we need Inheritance? (Need/Purpose)
 - Code reusability — common logic written once in the parent, reused by all children
 - Method overriding / runtime polymorphism — inheritance is a prerequisite for it
 - Establishes an IS-A relationship — Car IS-A Vehicle, modeling real-world hierarchies
 - Extensibility — new subclasses can be added without modifying existing code (Open/Closed Principle)
 - Maintainability — a fix in the parent automatically propagates to all subclasses
 
3]Types of Inheritance
	• Single Inheritance
		- Single inheritance is the concept of deriving the properties and behaviours from single base (parent) class
Ex:
class A{
	….
}
class  B extends A{
	….
}
	• Multilevel Inheritance
		- In the case of multi-level inheritance, a subclass that is inheriting one parent class will also act as the base class for another class.
Ex:
class A{
	….
}
class  B extends A{
	….
}
class  C extends B{
	….
}
	• Hierarchical Inheritance
		- In Hierarchical Inheritance concept, there is one base class for multiple subclasses.
Ex:
class A{
	….
}
class  B extends A{
	….
}
class  C extends A{
	….
}

	• Multiple Inheritance
		- Multiple Inheritance concept, there is more than one base classes for a subclass.

	• Hybrid Inheritance

Note:
 • Java supports Single, Multilevel and Hierarchical inheritance directly, but Multiple and Hybrid inheritance can be achieved through Interfaces.

4]Why doesn't Java support Multiple Inheritance through classes?
 - Because of the Diamond Problem — ambiguity when two parent classes provide the same method and the compiler can't determine 
 which implementation the child should inherit.
 Ex:
      A (method m())
     / \
    B   C  (both override m())
     \ /
      D    ← which m() does D inherit? Ambiguous.

5]What is the extends keyword? What about implements?
 - extends — used for class-to-class inheritance (only one allowed), and interface-to-interface inheritance (multiple allowed)
 - implements — used when a class implements an interface (multiple allowed)
 
6]What is the super keyword used for in inheritance?
Three uses:
 - super.method() — call the parent's version of an overridden method
 - super.field — access a parent field hidden by a same-named child field
 - super() — invoke the parent's constructor (must be the first statement)
Ex:
class Car extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    void start() {
        super.start();   // reuse parent logic
        System.out.println("...then Car-specific logic");
    }
}

7]Can an interface extend a class?
 - No. An interface can only extend other interfaces (one or many). 
 - A class can extend one class and implement many interfaces
 
8]What happens if the parent has only a parameterized constructor?
 - The child must explicitly call super(args), otherwise the implicit super() call fails to find a matching no-arg constructor 
 → compile-time error.

9]IS-A vs HAS-A relationship (Inheritance vs Composition)
 - IS-A (Inheritance) — Car IS-A Vehicle; achieved via extends/implements
 - HAS-A (Composition/Aggregation) — Car HAS-A Engine; achieved by holding a reference to another object as a field.
Ex:
class Car {
    private Engine engine;   // HAS-A relationship (composition)
}
